and criminal justice at Eastern Michigan University.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:12.590
It's really great having you on.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:14.019
I appreciate your time today.

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:15.110
Speaker 4: Thank you.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:21.490
Speaker 1: So in the book, you really do an
analysis of Trump and the Trump family and

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:24.330
the presidency and the business enterprise.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:30.170
Trump a through through sort of a criminology
lens, the idea of Trump operating as a sort

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.190
of pseudo mob boss, in a sense.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.840
Can you first lay out the kind of general
approach and perspective that you used here

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.900
and what and why you did it in this way?

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.329
Speaker 4: Okay.

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:48.809
Well, I'm a criminologist and I study crime
and I study the administration of justice.

00:00:48.809 --> 00:00:57.109
And I do so in a very broad based manner in
the sense that I examine all laws, whether

00:00:57.109 --> 00:01:00.239
they be civil or criminal.

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:01.570
Constitutional.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:02.900
International.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:08.140
So he was a perfect subject, a perfect object.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:15.640
And what what I think makes my analysis unique
or different from literally the thousands

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:25.200
of other books on Trump is that it is really
the first and only criminal biography of Donald

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:36.140
Trump, and it tries to give the 45th president
his rightful place in the history of U.S.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.900
crime.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:47.409
What it attempts to explain systemically or
systematically throughout his entire life

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:54.020
